![](/images/articles/cac-ltv.jpeg)

CAC/LTV is the weirdest metric in SaaS. It’s an unholy Frankenstein of a name. It has so many ingredients, it’s hard to calculate and harder to optimize. It’s difficult to pin on an accountable executive. It’s meaningful only on long timescales. It’s challenging to benchmark, because (as my friend[ Jeff Ignacio](https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeffbethechange/) has[ written](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/jeffbethechange_revenueoperations-startup-sales-activity-7140398137600278529-1OHo?utm_source=share&utm_medium=member_desktop)) it’s hard to standardize and vulnerable to gaming.

And it is indispensable.

CAC/LTV unites your go-to-market function around a clear goal: to build a good business. If you’re running a growth-stage¹ company, and you’re not thoughtful about CAC/LTV, you’re a hobbyist.

Don't worry about the math.² CAC/LTV is the return on your sales and marketing investment.³ The benchmark for CAC/LTV is generally regarded as 3.0x, meaning that for every dollar you spend to acquire a customer, that customer should generate three dollars of gross profit over its lifetime.

The problem with CAC/LTV comes from the weirdness of calculating lifetime value as customers expand, contract, and churn. Software products age and atrophy, inflation messes with the value of money, and we’re left with a tangled mess that no one really understands.

Why do I think it’s so important?

Go-to-market organizations are properly obsessed with months and quarters. Each period we ask ourselves: Did we make the sales number? Did we generate enough pipeline? Did we renew and up-sell existing customers?

But CAC/LTV asks a different question: Not “Did we have a good quarter?” but “Are we building a good business?” Consider what you need to achieve to sustain and improve this metric:

  - You need to achieve the sales numbers repeatably.
  - You need to run an efficient GTM machine.
  - You need to deliver and support customers profitably.
  - You need to make your customers happy, and to retain and grow your installed base.
  - You need to do all of these things sustainably, over the long term.

CAC/LTV forces your organization to be thoughtful about trade-offs. Marketing needs to generate demand, but are you generating quality opportunities? The sales team needs to make quota, but are you selling to customers who can be implemented profitably? The customer success team wants to make customers happy, but is that in balance with a reasonable cost structure? And how do those considerations flow back to the product?

I’ll never argue that CAC/LTV is the most important *quantitative* metric. It would be a terrible quarterly North Star. But to inform decision-making, to guide mindset and culture, and to shape a great business, I’ll never give it up.

¹CAC/LTV matters once you have a repeatable sales and marketing machine and your solution is mature enough to care about gross margins.

²You calculate CAC/LTV by taking the gross margin (profit) a customer generates over its lifetime divided by the cost spent to acquire that customer. But it's[ not quite that simple](https://www.wallstreetprep.com/knowledge/ltv-cac-ratio/#:~:text=The%20LTV%2FCAC%20ratio%20is%20computed%20by%20dividing%20the%20total,their%20first%20purchase%20(CAC).).

³I’d call it GTM/ROI but I’m not sure that’s better.[ The guys who came up with “magic number” need to get to work on naming](http://willprice.blogspot.com/2008/03/magic-number-for-saas-companies.html). I'm looking at you,[ Lars Leckie](https://www.linkedin.com/in/larsleckie/).
